					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/leicontis" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(393373); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=393373&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608843996" alt="Leicontis" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=393373)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/leicontis" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(393373); return false;" >Leicontis</a></span> <span class="odate time_1268327223 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Mar 2010 17:07</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-721257">
									

<p>Diamond does indeed burn well - if memory serves, carbon dioxide was first discovered by heating diamonds. However, there is no such thing as "more extensive" than a diamond, since diamond isn't a chemical compound but <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond_cubic">a bonding arrangement of carbon</a>, meaning that a flawless diamond is effectively monomolecular. Also, how does her skin have greater density than lead if it's made of a diamond-like carbon lattice? Lead is over 3x as dense as diamond, and about 20% denser than carbon could possibly achieve (if the atoms were packed as closely and efficiently as possible). That whole section of the description breaks immersion for me, but that's largely because materials science is my area of expertise - it's reasonable from a layman's perspective.</p>
<p>With the creator's permission, I'd like to try revising the parts of the description dealing with her composition.</p>

